    title: "Liver transplant recipient with a possible graft problem",
    safetyLevel: "warning",
    body: "**RULE**\n\u2022 Question 16 concerns peripheral blood biomarkers developed as a noninvasive alternative to liver biopsy: peripheral blood biomarkers alone should not be used to diagnose or exclude T-cell-mediated rejection and other forms of allograft injury (Strong, Level 4). [2]\n\u2022 Protocol liver biopsy is not recommended in the routine management of stable adult liver transplant recipients (Strong, Level 4). [2]\n\u2022 Not in the guideline: a stepwise approach to an abnormal liver test in a recipient, or a threshold that should trigger urgent action. [2]\n\n**INFECTION**\n\u2022 Donor-derived infection should be suspected in all early post-transplant infections (typically the first month), particularly with an unusual pathogen or atypical course, including unexplained fever, hepatitis or altered mental status. [3]\n\n**CALL**\n\u2022 Editor note: call the transplant team before changing, holding or stopping any immunosuppressant.\n\n**INTERACTIONS**\nApp content: the Sepsis consult's pneumonia regimen offers azithromycin as one atypical-coverage option.\n\u2022 Table 4 lists macrolide antibiotics among the drugs that raise calcineurin inhibitor levels. [2]\nRelated consults: [Sepsis](#/tree/sepsis) (fever, infection); [Altered mental status hub](#/tree/altered-mental-status-hub) (confusion); [Acute jaundice hub](#/tree/acute-jaundice-hub) (native liver); [Hepatitis](#/tree/hepatitis) (native liver); [CMV page](#/tree/transplant-patient-ed/node/tpe-cmv) (viral infection).\nDetail: [recognition detail](#/info/tpe-liver-recognition-detail).",
