    body: "**RULE**\n\u2022 MRCP should be used as the first-line diagnostic tool for anastomotic and non-anastomotic biliary strictures in adult liver transplant recipients (Strong, Level 2). [2]\n\u2022 An ERCP can be pursued, without a preceding MRCP or following a negative MRCP, when there is high clinical suspicion for biliary pathology or for the need of endoscopic intervention (Weak, Level 5). [2]\n\u2022 Duct-to-duct or choledochoduodenostomy anastomosis: endoscopic characterization, balloon dilation and stenting are first-line for anastomotic strictures (Strong, Level 2). [2]\n\u2022 Roux-en-Y choledochojejunostomy: either endoscopic stents or percutaneous transhepatic biliary drains, depending on the center's expertise (Strong, Level 3). [2]\n\u2022 Percutaneous transhepatic biliary drains should be reserved for strictures that have failed endoscopic management (Strong, Level 3). [2]\n\u2022 Percutaneous transhepatic biliary drain placement may be appropriate in patients with surgically altered foregut anatomy, such as a Roux-en-Y gastrojejunostomy, or who are critically ill with hemodynamic instability. [2]\n\u2022 Not in the guideline: timing, work-up or management for bile leak, or a cholangitis work-up. [2]\n\u2022 Table 2 names bile leak as a presentation of hepatic artery thrombosis. [2]\n\n**MORE**\nRelated consults: [Biliary pathology](#/tree/gallbladder/node/gb-cholangitis-severity) (cholangitis severity).\nDetail: [biliary detail](#/info/tpe-liver-biliary-detail).",
