    body: "\nKDIGO 2009, kidney recipients:\nEditor note: name suspected vascular compromise to the transplant team at the first call.\n\u2022 suggest kidney allograft ultrasound as part of assessing graft dysfunction (2C) [9]\n\u2022 ultrasound can diagnose arterial occlusion, venous thrombosis, urinary obstruction, urine leak, perinephric hematoma and post-biopsy arteriovenous fistula (rationale) [9]\n\u2022 causes needing rapid treatment: acute rejection, obstruction, urine leak, vascular compromise, some recurrent diseases such as FSGS (rationale) [9]\n\u2022 those rapid-treatment causes are more common in the first days to weeks after transplant than in later months to years (rationale) [9]\n\u2022 when hypertension is hard to control, especially with unexplained graft dysfunction, consider screening for vascular compromise (within or proximal to the allograft artery) (rationale) [9]\n\u2022 Not in KDIGO 2009: treatment of ureteric obstruction, urine leak, renal artery stenosis or graft thrombosis. Nearest: screening for allograft vascular compromise usually needs an angiogram, CT angiography or magnetic resonance imaging of the allograft vasculature (rationale) [9]\n[Details](#/info/tpe-kidney-vascular-detail)\nRelated consults: [Hypertensive Emergency (Adult)](#/tree/hypertensive-emergency-adult/node/hypertensive-emergency-adult-start) (severe hypertension); [Adult UTI](#/tree/adult-uti/node/uti-pyelonephritis) (pyelonephritis care).",
