    body: "\nKDIGO 2009, kidney recipients:\n\u2022 allopurinol with azathioprine can cause profound, life-threatening pancytopenia; use with extreme caution or not at all (rationale) [9]\n\u2022 NSAIDs and COX-2 inhibitors can significantly reduce kidney function and cause acute kidney injury (rationale) [9]\n\u2022 suggest colchicine for acute gout, with dose reduction for reduced kidney function and concomitant CNI use (2D) [9]\n\u2022 colchicine levels may be raised in reduced kidney function and with cyclosporine, and presumably tacrolimus (rationale) [9]\n\u2022 dose: in patients with eGFR under 60, avoid prolonged colchicine and avoid doses above 0.6 mg/day (rationale) [9]\n\u2022 rifampin strongly induces enzymes that metabolize CNIs and mTOR inhibitors, so adequate levels may be hard to keep (rationale) [9]\n\u2022 Not in KDIGO 2009: cautions for macrolides, aminoglycosides or vancomycin. Nearest: the guideline notes interactions between fluconazole and CNIs, and a report of a possible clotrimazole-tacrolimus interaction (rationale) [9]\n[Details](#/info/tpe-kidney-drugs-detail); [Tacrolimus and cyclosporine labels](#/tree/transplant-patient-ed/node/tpe-cni-drugs)\nRelated consults: [Sepsis](#/tree/sepsis/node/sepsis-start) (fever, shock).",
