    safetyLevel: "warning",
    summary: "CMV consensus 2025: send CMV PCR on plasma or whole blood; histology is recommended for tissue-invasive disease; culture of blood, urine, stool or oral secretions is not recommended.",
    body: "\u2022 CMV QNAT (PCR) calibrated to the WHO standard, on plasma or whole blood, is the recommended test (strong, high); keep the same specimen type and assay when monitoring (strong, high). [17]\n\u2022 No universal viral-load threshold for treatment has been established; centers set their own (strong, moderate). [17]\n\u2022 Not recommended: viral culture of blood, urine, stool or oral secretions (strong, high); QNAT on urine, stool or oral secretions (strong, low). Serology has no role. [17]\n\u2022 **Blood CMV DNA can be low or undetectable in gastrointestinal disease and, rarely, in lung recipients with CMV pneumonia.** [17]\n\u2022 **Organ disease:** histology with immunohistochemistry is recommended (strong, moderate). CMV DNA in CSF (assuming no blood contamination) likely represents disease; retinitis is diagnosed by eye examination. [17]\n\n[Test details, thresholds, organ-by-organ diagnosis](#/info/tpe-cmv-test)\n\nEditor note: send the CMV QNAT from the ED and tell the transplant team which specimen type (plasma or whole blood) was sent, because the guideline asks that monitoring use the same specimen type and assay.",
