    body: "\u2022 Without concomitant rejection, reducing immunosuppression is suggested in severe disease, inadequate response, high viral loads and cytopenia (weak, very low) (call the transplant team first). [17]\n\u2022 Not in the guideline: how much or which drug to reduce, or what to do if rejection coexists. Nearest sourced fact: optimization of immunosuppression and drug dosing is recommended as feasible in resistant or refractory CMV (strong, low). [17]\n\u2022 Suspect resistance with prior cumulative antiviral exposure exceeding 4 weeks and clinical failure despite at least 2 weeks of appropriately dosed treatment (strong, moderate); genotyping is recommended after that exposure and response (strong, high). [17]\n\u2022 Maribavir or foscarnet are recommended second-line treatments when valganciclovir or ganciclovir is not tolerated (strong, low); maribavir is the principal alternative in resistance to ganciclovir or foscarnet (strong, high). Avoid maribavir in CNS disease or retinitis (flowchart footnote, not graded). [17]\n\u2022 The two guidelines give different viral-load markers for foscarnet: see the resistance page.\n\n[Resistance, second-line drugs, source conflicts](#/info/tpe-cmv-resistance)\n[Immunosuppression, rejection, IVIG, secondary prophylaxis, letermovir](#/info/tpe-cmv-team)\n\nRelated consults: [Calcineurin inhibitor toxicity](#/tree/transplant-patient-ed/node/tpe-cni-toxicity) tacrolimus, cyclosporine.",
